even as many nations around the world  struggle with covet vaccine shortages  india is distributing millions of doses  in its neighborhood  and further afield most have been given  away free  some commercially the policy is in line  with prime minister narendra modi's  assurance  to make affordable vaccines available to  the world  but there are definite hints of indian  soft power at play  especially at a time that china too is  ramping up its own  so-called vaccine diplomacy  this is the first consignment of covert  19 vaccine to arrive in afghanistan  it includes half a million doses and was  donated by the government of india  the international press and a host of  vips have been assembled to witness the  event  so what's behind such generosity an  embassy spokesperson offers one  explanation  india is very happy that you know we  have a long tradition of history and  cooperation  in good times and bad india and  afghanistan have stayed together  and we call it as a vaccine my three  which is you know  friendship through vaccination  friendship  through vaccination while other  countries are accused of hoarding  vaccines  or failing to secure enough for their  citizens  india has already given away more than 5  million doses  and may donate as many as 20 million to  neighboring nations in the near future  among the beneficiaries are myanmar  bangladesh  sri lanka bhutan and nepal  we have been successful in bringing the  vaccines to nepal  for this i would like to thank the  indian government prime minister  narendra modi and the people of india on  behalf of nepal's government  prime minister k.p sharma ali and the  people of nepal  india is one of the world's largest  vaccine producers  already meeting more than 60 per cent of  global demand  one of its champions is the privately  owned serum institute of india  when covert 19 hit they teamed up with  british swedish firm  astrazeneca together they quickly  produced and stockpiled more than 70  million doses  which put india in a position to be able  to help other countries with their  inoculation drives  for india this is a source of national  pride  it's also seen as a way of improving  ties in the region  and some say leveraging any goodwill  that might be generated  others suggest delhi is hoping to  counter chinese influence as beijing  conducts its own vaccine diplomacy  delivering doses of its jab to countries  in india's backyard  joining me now is anand krishnan he's  china correspondent for the hindu  newspaper  and author of the book india's china  challenge he joins me now from chennai  anant welcome  now india calls it vaccine matri or  vaccine friendship  but is it primarily a way to counter  chinese influence  in what india has always seen as its  area of influence  well baris i don't uh really think so  because if you look at  the spread of where india has been  sending its vaccines it's of course  focused in the immediate neighborhood  bangladesh  myanmar and nepal have received more  doses from india than any other country  but india is also supplying vaccines to  egypt south africa  brazil now countries in the caribbean i  think it's more a question of india  happening to be one of the places in the  world that has the biggest capacity  for making vaccines and right now birsh  the it's more a question of demand and  supply  where india is going to be producing way  more than it needs  uh just so far uh india has vaccinated  about eight million people  and it has sent about 16 million doses  of vaccines abroad  of which about uh one-third were grants  in the rest on a commercial basis  so i think relation is more a demand  supply issue rather than a vaccine  competition and  as someone in nepal recently told me if  india and china are going head over  heels to supply vaccines it's a good  thing for the world  but is there an element of software here  that india is hoping to project  for sure i'm sure that is an added  element i don't think it would be the  case where  diplomacy or geopolitics is driving this  effort i think as i said it's more a  question of india having the capacity to  do so  but of course i think for india for  china for every other country there is  an added element  as you can see india's external affairs  minister has been tweeting almost every  dispatch  uh the indian envoys and the indian  missions and the countries that  receiving them have been quite active on  social media showcasing it  so of course as you said i think it's a  wonderful opportunity to showcase soft  power for india for china for any other  country  but my own sense is the idea of vaccine  competition between india and china is  something i think  perhaps being played up too much in the  media i think china itself has two  vaccines that it's producing  in large quantities as you know they've  controlled  the coronavirus spread in china so they  do have the capacity to export them  overseas as well so i think it is a good  thing for the world that india  and china are emerging as big suppliers  of vaccines  india especially being one of the  world's biggest manufacturers it's no  doubt a good thing for the world  and if there is some element of soft  power competition i think bereis so be  it  well let's just for try and focus on the  soft part i'm thinking particularly of  india's ties in the immediate  neighborhood  with countries such as bangladesh and  nepal which have generally been fraught  in the recent past do you think that  accidentally if i can put it that way  they might see a slight improvement  i think it is interesting where it seems  that india's relations with the  immediate neighborhood seems to be  cyclical  where they go through periods of crisis  and then they kind of patch up and i  think that happens a lot especially with  nepal and sri lanka  you've seen them gravitate towards china  and then you've had changes in  government they  gravitate back towards india my feeling  is i think there is some  amount of realism in delhi especially if  you have smaller countries in your  immediate neighborhood it's in their  interest  to try and play india off of china to  get the best deal i don't think that  dynamic is going to change  and i would expect that push pull to  continue in the immediate neighborhood  for some time to come  i think uh bresh we should keep in mind  that there's a spectrum  in if you look at india's immediate  neighborhood i think that ranges  from bhutan on the one end which has  extremely close relations with india no  ties with china  to obviously pakistan at the other end i  think in between it's a lot of  grey area i think if you look at  bangladesh nepal sri lanka the maldives  they seem to be moving to china and then  away from china and i think that  kind of dynamic is here to stay and i  think you're seeing that reflected a  little bit in the vaccines as well  it's quite interesting that china has  been quite slow to be able to send its  vaccines for whatever reason  two countries in south asia barring  pakistan china has very belatedly  announced a half a million  deal to supply vaccines to nepal and  there was some controversy in nepal  apparently leaking documents saying with  the chinese embassy there was pressuring  nepal to accept to  kind of take up chinese vaccines the  chinese media has accused india of  blocking  chinese trials in bangladesh although  later it emerged was more about  the chinese company changing the terms  of the deal so as you said british i  think it is playing out a little bit  but perhaps a little bit too much being  made of it  in my opinion anand krishnan thanks very  much for sharing that opinion  you
 

